Define the tool as a **function schema**, send it with the model request, and use **`tool_choice="auto"`** so the model can either request Python execution or answer directly. Your application—not the model—executes the requested code.

This follows the article’s central distinction: **“The model decides what should happen. The harness controls how it happens.”** [Source: Agents Zero to Hero #1](https://nik-hil.hashnode.dev/agents-zero-to-hero-1-building-an-ai-agent-from-scratch-with-tool-calling)

### 1. Define the Python execution schema

The article uses a tool named `execute_code`, with one required string argument, `code`:

```python
TOOL_SCHEMAS = [
    {
        "type": "function",
        "function": {
            "name": "execute_code",
            "description": "Execute Python code and return stdout or error.",
            "parameters": {
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                    "code": {
                        "type": "string",
                        "description": "The Python code to execute"
                    }
                },
                "required": ["code"],
                "additionalProperties": False
            }
        }
    }
]
```

The schema describes the interface; it does **not** implement or run Python. You must separately supply an `execute_code(code: str)` implementation.

